# The first divisions of C. elegans decided by who touches whom, not by who is named.
#
# This is founders.bio with one change of kind. Its three signals named their sender and their
# receiver (`from: cell = P2; to: cell = ABp`), so the geometry of the embryo was written into the
# rule and could not be wrong. Here a signal names neither cell. A receiver reads the amount of
# ligand summed over the cells that touch it at that moment (BioLang v0.4 §7.4, `mode: contact;
# reads: amount`); who touches it comes from contacts_embryo.tsv; which cells present a ligand at
# all is an `express` decision carrying the measurement that showed it; and which cells can hear a
# Delta is the maternal GLP-1 protein, inherited by the AB lineage.
#
# ABp therefore differs from ABa here for the reason it differs in the embryo: ABp touches P2 and
# ABa does not. Move that contact in the table and the fates move with it, which is the experiment
# Priess & Thomson 1987 and Hutter & Schnabel 1994 did by rearranging blastomeres, and which
# founders.bio cannot state at all. scripts/celegans_contacts.py runs it.
#
# Two things here are workarounds, not biology, and are marked as such below:
#   * `wake_at_new_delta` and `wake_at_new_wnt`: an amount is read only when the receiving cell
#     itself decides again, so a receiver born before its sender (ABp at 28 min, P2 at 42 min)
#     would never hear it. These two presence signals carry no geometry — their sender and receiver
#     conditions are states, not names — and exist only to make receivers decide again when a cell
#     that presents a ligand is born. The runtime change that would remove them is written down in
#     docs/ORGANISM-FROM-ONE-CELL.md.
#   * `EMSPolarised`: the Wnt contact is transient (P2 divides before EMS does), while the polarity
#     it establishes is not, so the read is latched into a cell type, which persists. That part is
#     biology (Goldstein 1992); needing a type rather than a state for it is the language's limit.
#
# Review: Maduro 2010, Dev Dyn 239:1315; Priess 2005, WormBook (Notch).
# test: entities >= 18
# test: unknowns == 0
module organism.celegans.founders_contacts
import bio.std.development

cell_type ABaPrecursor { parent: Blastomere; evidence: experimental "Priess & Thomson 1987, Cell 48:241 (ABa: anterior pharynx, neurons, hypodermis)"; confidence: 0.9 }
cell_type ABpPrecursor { parent: Blastomere; evidence: experimental "Priess & Thomson 1987 (ABp fates need the P2 signal)"; confidence: 0.9 }
cell_type EMSPrecursor { parent: Blastomere; evidence: experimental "Mello et al. 1992 (EMS: pharynx, intestine, muscle)"; confidence: 0.9 }
cell_type EMSPolarised { parent: EMSPrecursor; evidence: experimental "Goldstein 1992, Nature 357:255; Goldstein 1995, Development 121:1227 (the P2 contact polarises EMS, and is needed only before EMS divides)"; confidence: 0.85 }
cell_type MSPrecursor { parent: Blastomere; evidence: experimental "Sulston 1983 (MS: pharynx, body muscle, coelomocytes)"; confidence: 0.9 }
cell_type EPrecursor { parent: Blastomere; evidence: experimental "Sulston 1983 (E: the 20 intestinal cells)"; confidence: 0.9 }
cell_type CPrecursor { parent: Blastomere; evidence: experimental "Sulston 1983 (C: hypodermis, body muscle)"; confidence: 0.9 }
cell_type DPrecursor { parent: Blastomere; evidence: experimental "Sulston 1983 (D: 20 body muscle cells)"; confidence: 0.9 }

# --- which cells present a ligand: measured, and independent of who hears it ---------------------
decision presents_apx1 { action: express; when: cell = P2, PIE-1 = present; sets: APX-1; evidence: experimental "Mickey et al. 1996, Development 122:1791 (APX-1 acts in P2); Mello et al. 1994, Cell 77:95; Du et al. 2014, Cell 156:359 (without PIE-1 the P2 identity is lost and ABp becomes ABa-like)"; confidence: 0.9 }
decision presents_mom2 { action: express; when: cell = P2; sets: MOM-2; evidence: experimental "Thorpe et al. 1997, Cell 90:695; Rocheleau et al. 1997, Cell 90:707 (MOM-2 from P2)"; confidence: 0.9 }
decision presents_lag2 { action: express; when: cell = MS; sets: LAG-2; evidence: experimental "Priess 2005, WormBook (the second Notch interaction is signalled by MS)"; confidence: 0.8 }

# --- the signals: no sender named, no receiver named --------------------------------------------
# The amount is the ligand summed over the cells touching this one now, weighted by contact area.
signal Notch_at_contact { mode: contact; ligand: APX-1; receptor: GLP-1; reads: amount; from: APX-1 = present; sets: Notch_amount; evidence: experimental "Mello et al. 1994, Cell 77:95; Mickey et al. 1996, Development 122:1791"; confidence: 0.9 }
signal Wnt_at_contact { mode: contact; ligand: MOM-2; receptor: MOM-5; reads: amount; from: MOM-2 = present; sets: Wnt_amount; evidence: experimental "Thorpe et al. 1997, Cell 90:695; Rocheleau et al. 1997, Cell 90:707"; confidence: 0.9 }
signal Notch2_at_contact { mode: contact; ligand: LAG-2; receptor: GLP-1; reads: amount; from: LAG-2 = present; sets: Notch2_amount; evidence: experimental "Priess 2005, WormBook (second Notch interaction, anterior pharynx from ABa)"; confidence: 0.8 }

# a runtime workaround, not a mechanism: these make a cell decide again when a cell that presents a
# ligand is born, so that the amounts above are read. They carry no geometry, and nothing reads what
# they set; every statement about who touches whom is in contacts_embryo.tsv.
signal wake_at_new_delta { mode: contact; ligand: APX-1; receptor: GLP-1; from: APX-1 = present; to: GLP-1 = present; sets: delta_presenter_exists; evidence: inferred "a workaround: the Body reads a contact amount only when the receiver decides again"; confidence: 0.1 }
signal wake_at_new_wnt { mode: contact; ligand: MOM-2; receptor: MOM-5; from: MOM-2 = present; to: MOM-5 = present; sets: wnt_presenter_exists; evidence: inferred "a workaround: the Body reads a contact amount only when the receiver decides again"; confidence: 0.1 }

# --- divisions: which daughter keeps which maternal factor --------------------------------------
# GLP-1 is maternal and its protein is restricted to the AB lineage, which is why only AB cells can
# hear a Delta at a contact: the restriction is inheritance, not a name in the signal.
decision div_P0 { action: divide; when: cell = P0; daughters: AB, P1; lineages: AB = AB, P1 = P; after: 0 min; asymmetric: PIE-1 -> P1, SKN-1 -> P1, PAL-1 -> P1, PAR-2 -> P1, PAR-3 -> AB, GLP-1 -> AB; evidence: experimental "Sulston 1983; Kemphues et al. 1988, Cell 52:311 (PAR); Mello 1992 (PIE-1); Bowerman 1992 (SKN-1); Evans et al. 1994, Cell 77:183 (GLP-1 protein in AB only)"; confidence: 0.9 }
decision div_P1 { action: divide; when: cell = P1; daughters: EMS, P2; lineages: EMS = EMS, P2 = P; asymmetric: PIE-1 -> P2; evidence: experimental "Sulston 1983; Mello et al. 1992, Cell 70:163 (PIE-1 to the germline side; SKN-1 and PAL-1 in both)"; confidence: 0.9 }
decision div_EMS_polarised { action: divide; when: cell_type = EMSPolarised; daughters: MS, E; lineages: MS = MS, E = E; asymmetric: POP-1 -> MS; evidence: experimental "Sulston 1983; Lin et al. 1998, Cell 92:229 (the Wnt signal lowers POP-1 in the posterior daughter, E)"; confidence: 0.9 }
decision div_EMS { action: divide; when: cell = EMS; daughters: MS, E; lineages: MS = MS, E = E; evidence: experimental "Thorpe et al. 1997; Rocheleau et al. 1997 (with no Wnt at the contact both daughters keep POP-1 high and E takes the MS fate)"; confidence: 0.9 }
decision div_P2 { action: divide; when: cell = P2; daughters: C, P3; lineages: C = C, P3 = P; asymmetric: PIE-1 -> P3, SKN-1 -> P3; evidence: experimental "Sulston 1983; SKN-1 is not maintained in C; PAL-1 in both (Hunter & Kenyon 1996, Cell 87:217)"; confidence: 0.8 }
decision div_P3 { action: divide; when: cell = P3; daughters: D, P4; lineages: D = D, P4 = P4; asymmetric: PIE-1 -> P4, SKN-1 -> P4; evidence: experimental "Sulston 1983; Hunter & Kenyon 1996"; confidence: 0.8 }

# --- founder fates from the factors each cell holds and the ligand it hears ----------------------
# ABa and ABp are one rule: the only thing that separates them is how much Delta each hears.
decision fate_ABp { action: differentiate; when: cell = ABa|ABp, GLP-1 = present, Notch_amount = >=0.5; to: ABpPrecursor; evidence: experimental "Mello et al. 1994, Cell 77:95 (APX-1/GLP-1); Hutter & Schnabel 1994, Development 120:2051 (the fate follows the contact, not the lineage)"; confidence: 0.9 }
decision fate_ABa { action: differentiate; when: cell = ABa|ABp; to: ABaPrecursor; evidence: experimental "Priess & Thomson 1987, Cell 48:241; Mello et al. 1994, Cell 77:95 (ABa-like is what an AB daughter is when it hears no Delta: the rule above takes precedence when it does)"; confidence: 0.9 }
decision fate_EMS_polarised { action: differentiate; when: cell_type = EMSPrecursor, Wnt_amount = >=0.5; to: EMSPolarised; evidence: experimental "Goldstein 1992, Nature 357:255 (the P2 contact polarises EMS); Thorpe et al. 1997, Cell 90:695"; confidence: 0.85 }
decision fate_EMS { action: differentiate; when: cell = EMS|P2, SKN-1 = present, PIE-1 = absent; to: EMSPrecursor; evidence: experimental "Bowerman et al. 1992, Cell 68:1061 (SKN-1); Mello et al. 1992 (without PIE-1, P2 takes the EMS fate)"; confidence: 0.9 }
decision fate_MS { action: differentiate; when: cell = MS|E, SKN-1 = present, POP-1 = present; to: MSPrecursor; evidence: experimental "Lin et al. 1995, Cell 83:599; Broitman-Maduro et al. 2006 (TBX-35)"; confidence: 0.9 }
decision fate_E { action: differentiate; when: cell = MS|E, SKN-1 = present, POP-1 = absent; to: EPrecursor; evidence: experimental "Lin et al. 1995 (without POP-1, MS takes the E fate); Maduro et al. 2005 (END-1/END-3)"; confidence: 0.9 }
decision fate_C { action: differentiate; when: cell = MS|E|C, SKN-1 = absent, PAL-1 = present; to: CPrecursor; evidence: experimental "Hunter & Kenyon 1996, Cell 87:217 (PAL-1 for C; without SKN-1, EMS daughters take the C fate)"; confidence: 0.9 }
decision fate_D { action: differentiate; when: cell = D, PAL-1 = present, SKN-1 = absent; to: DPrecursor; evidence: experimental "Hunter & Kenyon 1996 (PAL-1 for D)"; confidence: 0.8 }
decision germline { action: differentiate; when: cell = P4, PIE-1 = present; to: GermCell; evidence: experimental "Mello et al. 1996, Nature 382:710 (PIE-1 keeps the germline transcriptionally silent)"; confidence: 0.9 }
